#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084040} :activity/id #uuid "3329496d-4743-5f4d-aa65-19187c9c8e23" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049707 :topic/id #uuid "0d118f55-a6b1-5729-9c06-3dbae669ed79" :topic/math-academy-id 754} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084518 :tutorial/content "The **range** of a function is the collection of numbers (or set) containing all the function's outputs.\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$, defined by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/60/60ae5361f9f51bb7e61083269770ee3dd432e04ad2f968df07d78c8dc61e7188.png)\n\nIn a mapping diagram, the range consists of all the outputs to which an arrow is drawn. Therefore, the range is\n\n$$\n\\{-1,2,3 \\}\n$$\n\nThe value $y=0$ is not part of the range because there is no $x$-value such that\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a6969f16-6b56-552e-8162-ba459b815dc6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8799 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2642b03f-30f8-5a73-8a08-f51f7b592baf" :step/math-academy-id 150950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083178 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082939 :question/id #uuid "9e732b25-cc9a-5a1a-b6d8-3502495a7fe4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14165"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "390282db-587c-5b6c-a051-f62e4092bb0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083677}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Function's Range From a Mapping Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "4f14b6dd-48cf-50bf-9faa-ac12c6ebb738" :step/math-academy-id 150951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082940 :question/id #uuid "0b107cc8-8085-57b4-b194-9adc02698826" :question/math-academy-id "e-14170"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d3796fa-e911-5553-83c2-24c98370e4ea" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083936}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Function's Range From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "5f681af9-5dd3-5ccc-8663-eb8759acc874" :step/math-academy-id 150954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084519 :tutorial/content "When we graph a function, the range is the set of all $y$-values given by the graph.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x\n$$\n\ngraphed below.\n\n![](images/09/09716693ba950bd3d14d0ea008dbd9d99dc51a26f30e87ccbf1559f34329fed9.png)\n\nThe graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty < y< \\infty\n$$\n\nwhich we can also write as\n$-\\infty < f(x) < \\infty$.\n\nFurthermore, we can express the range as $f(x)\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ using interval notation.\n\nIf we restrict the domain, the range also changes. Let's now consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x, \\quad x \\in [0,2)\n$$\n\nThe graph $y = g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/791aca2b232c0eee5950c4c1c87073b4c51cd5d61d094de893ca4a2ec28e8d1e.png)\n\nThe $y$ values go from $y=0$ up to (but not including) $y=4$, including every value in between. Therefore, the range of the function is\n\n$$\n0\\leq g(x) < 4\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $g(x)\\in [0,4)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "35906cd0-d08e-5f2a-b88e-9e8ac1329502" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8801 :tutorial/title "Graphs of Functions"} :step/id #uuid "0e0d7002-1126-53ab-8ffd-a09dbe4a234a" :step/math-academy-id 150957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083180 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082941 :question/id #uuid "6b1dabe1-f820-5ab8-aceb-bbd5be3029a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14172"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4d624bf-dfd2-5910-a5a6-c72352e76e40" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083937}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Function's Range From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "5bd906c2-2b91-5274-869f-0425ba950919" :step/math-academy-id 150958}] :activity/title "The Range of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083444}]}
